      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "24a7ec0c-e7a9-5612-895d-0a95e91bf0bf",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Alexander Borodin\n- Works: Stranger in Paradise, Polovtsian Dances, Prince Igor, Gliding Dance of the Maidens\n- Concepts: Polovtsy, Kipchaks, Cumans\n\nFacts:\n- Alexander Borodin composed the opera Prince Igor, which dates to 1890.\n- The Polovtsian Dances form an exotic scene at the end of Act II of Alexander Borodin's opera Prince Igor.\n- The Russian title of the Polovtsian Dances is \"Половецкие пляски\" and is transliterated as \"Polovetskie plyaski.\"\n- The term \"Polovtsy\" is the Rus' name for the Kipchaks and Cumans.\n- The song Stranger in Paradise uses a melody taken from Alexander Borodin's \"Gliding Dance of the Maidens,\" which is part of the Polovtsian Dances in Prince Igor.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "2b419f4a-7245-55bd-b90f-47d4f9ff2b79",
        "text": "Page: Stranger in Paradise (song). Sentence: Like almost all the music in that show, the melody was taken from music composed by Alexander Borodin (1833–1887), in this case, the \"Gliding Dance of the Maidens\", from the \"Polovtsian Dances\" in the opera \"Prince Igor\" (1890).\nPage: Polovtsian Dances. Sentence: The Polovtsian Dances, or Polovetsian Dances (Russian: Половецкие пляски, \"Polovetskie plyaski\" from the Russian \"Polovtsy\"—the name given to the Kipchaks and Cumans by the Rus' people) form an exotic scene at the end of Act II of Alexander Borodin's opera \"Prince Igor\".",
